(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("data-platform",{"data-platform/lakes-warehouses-and-lakehouses-architecture":{"html":"\u003cp>A data platform moves data from the systems that create it to the people and models that use it. The shape of the storage layer decides cost, speed and how painful change will be.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Sources, ingestion, storage, processing and serving\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Warehouse, lake and lakehouse compared\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Open table formats: Iceberg and Delta\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Zones: raw, cleaned and curated\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Batch and streaming paths\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layers\">Layers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Sources are applications, databases and event streams. Ingestion copies them into storage with tools such as change data capture or file loads. Processing cleans and joins data. Serving exposes tables to analysts, dashboards and models. Each layer should be observable and owned by a named team.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"warehouse-lake-lakehouse\">Warehouse, lake, lakehouse\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A warehouse stores structured tables with strong performance and governance, but storage and compute are often tied together. A lake keeps files of any shape in cheap object storage, but without rules it becomes a swamp. A lakehouse puts table semantics, transactions and schema on top of object storage so one copy of data serves both styles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"table-formats\">Table formats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Apache Iceberg and Delta Lake add a metadata layer over files in object storage. They give atomic commits, schema evolution, time travel and file level pruning. Without such a format, two jobs writing the same folder can leave readers with half written data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"zones\">Zones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A common layout has a raw zone that keeps data exactly as received, a cleaned zone with typed and deduplicated tables, and a curated zone with business ready models. Raw data is never edited, so any later layer can be rebuilt after a bug is fixed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"batch-and-streaming\">Batch and streaming\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Batch jobs process large sets on a schedule and are simple and cheap. Streaming processes events as they arrive and gives fresh results at higher complexity. Many platforms use batch for most tables and streaming only where freshness has real value.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"src&gt;ing;ing&gt;lake;lake&gt;srv\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Lakes, warehouses and lakehouses: architecture\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"src\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ing\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ingestion\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lake\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object storage tables\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"srv\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Serving layer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Sources to Ingestion; 2, Ingestion to Object storage tables; 3, Object storage tables to Serving layer.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"src\">\u003cdt>Sources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Apps and databases.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Schema change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ing\">\u003cdt>Ingestion \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> CDC and loads.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lag.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lake\">\u003cdt>Object storage tables \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Iceberg or Delta.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Small files.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"srv\">\u003cdt>Serving layer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Marts and APIs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-dashboard-that-showed-half-of-yesterday\">Incident: A dashboard that showed half of yesterday\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two jobs wrote to the same folder and a report read partial files.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws s3 ls s3://corp-lake/curated/orders/dt=2026-11-20/ --summarize | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws s3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> s3://corp-lake/curated/orders/dt=2026-11-20/ --summarize | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Total Objects: 187\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   Total Size: 4.1 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A compaction job and a load job wrote to the same partition while a dashboard query ran, so the report counted some files twice and missed others. Moving the tables to Iceberg gave atomic commits, so readers see either the old snapshot or the new one, never a mix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing files straight into folders that others read\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing raw data in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for each table\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Everything streaming when batch is enough\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Many tiny files that slow every query\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layers","text":"Layers"},{"id":"warehouse-lake-lakehouse","text":"Warehouse, lake, lakehouse"},{"id":"table-formats","text":"Table formats"},{"id":"zones","text":"Zones"},{"id":"batch-and-streaming","text":"Batch and streaming"},{"id":"incident-a-dashboard-that-showed-half-of-yesterday","text":"Incident: A dashboard that showed half of yesterday"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Apache Iceberg documentation","u":"https://iceberg.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"t":"Delta Lake","u":"https://github.com/delta-io/delta"},{"t":"Data mesh principles","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/data-mesh-principles.html"}],"text":"A data platform moves data from the systems that create it to the people and models that use it. The shape of the storage layer decides cost, speed and how painful change will be. What you will learn Sources, ingestion, storage, processing and serving Warehouse, lake and lakehouse compared Open table formats: Iceberg and Delta Zones: raw, cleaned and curated Batch and streaming paths Layers Sources are applications, databases and event streams. Ingestion copies them into storage with tools such as change data capture or file loads. Processing cleans and joins data. Serving exposes tables to analysts, dashboards and models. Each layer should be observable and owned by a named team. Warehouse, lake, lakehouse A warehouse stores structured tables with strong performance and governance, but storage and compute are often tied together. A lake keeps files of any shape in cheap object storage, but without rules it becomes a swamp. A lakehouse puts table semantics, transactions and schema on top of object storage so one copy of data serves both styles. Table formats Apache Iceberg and Delta Lake add a metadata layer over files in object storage. They give atomic commits, schema evolution, time travel and file level pruning. Without such a format, two jobs writing the same folder can leave readers with half written data. Zones A common layout has a raw zone that keeps data exactly as received, a cleaned zone with typed and deduplicated tables, and a curated zone with business ready models. Raw data is never edited, so any later layer can be rebuilt after a bug is fixed. Batch and streaming Batch jobs process large sets on a schedule and are simple and cheap. Streaming processes events as they arrive and gives fresh results at higher complexity. Many platforms use batch for most tables and streaming only where freshness has real value. Lakes, warehouses and lakehouses: architecture Flow in order: 1, Sources to Ingestion; 2, Ingestion to Object storage tables; 3, Object storage tables to Serving layer. Outside party Step Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Sources Outside party What it does: Apps and databases. If it fails or is weak: Schema change. Ingestion Step What it does: CDC and loads. If it fails or is weak: Lag. Object storage tables Data store What it does: Iceberg or Delta. If it fails or is weak: Small files. Serving layer Service What it does: Marts and APIs. If it fails or is weak: Stale data. Incident: A dashboard that showed half of yesterday Incident Two jobs wrote to the same folder and a report read partial files. $ aws s3 ls s3://corp-lake/curated/orders/dt=2026-11-20/ --summarize | tail -2 Total Objects: 187 Total Size: 4.1 GiB (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A compaction job and a load job wrote to the same partition while a dashboard query ran, so the report counted some files twice and missed others. Moving the tables to Iceberg gave atomic commits, so readers see either the old snapshot or the new one, never a mix. Common mistakes Writing files straight into folders that others read Editing raw data in place No owner for each table Everything streaming when batch is enough Many tiny files that slow every query"},"data-platform/kafka-operations-topics-partitions-and-consumer-groups":{"html":"\u003cp>Kafka is the backbone of many data platforms. It is reliable when its settings match the promise you make to producers and consumers, and painful when they do not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Topics, partitions and ordering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Replication, acks and min.insync.replicas\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Consumer groups, offsets and lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention and compaction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity and rebalancing\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"topics-and-partitions\">Topics and partitions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A topic is split into partitions, and order is guaranteed only inside one partition. Messages with the same key go to the same partition, so choose keys that keep related events together. The number of partitions limits how many consumers in a group can read in parallel and is hard to reduce later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"durability-settings\">Durability settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each partition has a leader and followers. With \u003ccode>acks=all\u003c/code> a write is confirmed after the in sync replicas have it. Set \u003ccode>replication.factor=3\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>min.insync.replicas=2\u003c/code> so the loss of one broker neither loses data nor stops writes. With \u003ccode>acks=1\u003c/code> a leader crash can lose acknowledged messages.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"consumer-groups-and-lag\">Consumer groups and lag\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Consumers in a group share partitions, and each commits the offset it has processed. Lag is the gap between the newest message and the committed offset. Rising lag means consumers are too slow, stuck or rebalancing, so alert on lag and on its trend.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-compaction\">Retention and compaction\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Retention deletes data by age or size, for example seven days. Log compaction keeps the latest value for each key and suits changelog topics. A consumer that is down longer than the retention period loses messages it never read.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"capacity-and-rebalancing\">Capacity and rebalancing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Watch disk, network and request latency per broker. Adding brokers does not move data by itself, so run a partition reassignment with throttling to avoid starving normal traffic. Spread leaders evenly and keep brokers in different racks or zones.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;brk;brk&gt;grp;grp&gt;mon\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Kafka operations: topics, partitions and consumer groups\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"brk\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Broker cluster\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"grp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Consumer group\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lag and ISR alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producers to Broker cluster; 2, Broker cluster to Consumer group; 3, Consumer group to Lag and ISR alerts.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Send with keys.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> acks=1.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"brk\">\u003cdt>Broker cluster \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Replicated partitions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Under replicated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"grp\">\u003cdt>Consumer group \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads and commits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lag.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Lag and ISR alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Detect trouble.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not watched.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-consumers-fell-behind-and-then-lost-messages\">Incident: Consumers fell behind and then lost messages\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A consumer group was down over a weekend and its offsets expired from the topic.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server broker1:9092 --describe --group billing\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server broker1:9092 --describe --group billing\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">GROUP    TOPIC    PARTITION  CURRENT-OFFSET  LOG-END-OFFSET  LAG\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">billing  invoices 0          0               482113          482113\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Retention on the topic was two days and the group was down for three, so the oldest messages were deleted before they were read and the group restarted from the earliest available offset. The team raised retention to seven days for billing topics and added an alert on lag older than one hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>acks=1 for important data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>min.insync.replicas of 1\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Too few partitions to scale consumers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No alert on consumer lag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reassigning partitions without a throttle\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"topics-and-partitions","text":"Topics and partitions"},{"id":"durability-settings","text":"Durability settings"},{"id":"consumer-groups-and-lag","text":"Consumer groups and lag"},{"id":"retention-and-compaction","text":"Retention and compaction"},{"id":"capacity-and-rebalancing","text":"Capacity and rebalancing"},{"id":"incident-consumers-fell-behind-and-then-lost-messages","text":"Incident: Consumers fell behind and then lost messages"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Apache Kafka documentation","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/"},{"t":"Kafka operations","u":"https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/#operations"},{"t":"Apache Iceberg documentation","u":"https://iceberg.apache.org/docs/latest/"}],"text":"Kafka is the backbone of many data platforms. It is reliable when its settings match the promise you make to producers and consumers, and painful when they do not. What you will learn Topics, partitions and ordering Replication, acks and min.insync.replicas Consumer groups, offsets and lag Retention and compaction Capacity and rebalancing Topics and partitions A topic is split into partitions, and order is guaranteed only inside one partition. Messages with the same key go to the same partition, so choose keys that keep related events together. The number of partitions limits how many consumers in a group can read in parallel and is hard to reduce later. Durability settings Each partition has a leader and followers. With acks=all a write is confirmed after the in sync replicas have it. Set replication.factor=3 and min.insync.replicas=2 so the loss of one broker neither loses data nor stops writes. With acks=1 a leader crash can lose acknowledged messages. Consumer groups and lag Consumers in a group share partitions, and each commits the offset it has processed. Lag is the gap between the newest message and the committed offset. Rising lag means consumers are too slow, stuck or rebalancing, so alert on lag and on its trend. Retention and compaction Retention deletes data by age or size, for example seven days. Log compaction keeps the latest value for each key and suits changelog topics. A consumer that is down longer than the retention period loses messages it never read. Capacity and rebalancing Watch disk, network and request latency per broker. Adding brokers does not move data by itself, so run a partition reassignment with throttling to avoid starving normal traffic. Spread leaders evenly and keep brokers in different racks or zones. Kafka operations: topics, partitions and consumer groups Flow in order: 1, Producers to Broker cluster; 2, Broker cluster to Consumer group; 3, Consumer group to Lag and ISR alerts. Outside party Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producers Outside party What it does: Send with keys. If it fails or is weak: acks=1. Broker cluster Data store What it does: Replicated partitions. If it fails or is weak: Under replicated. Consumer group Step What it does: Reads and commits. If it fails or is weak: Lag. Lag and ISR alerts Observability What it does: Detect trouble. If it fails or is weak: Not watched. Incident: Consumers fell behind and then lost messages Incident A consumer group was down over a weekend and its offsets expired from the topic. $ kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server broker1:9092 --describe --group billing GROUP TOPIC PARTITION CURRENT-OFFSET LOG-END-OFFSET LAG billing invoices 0 0 482113 482113 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) Retention on the topic was two days and the group was down for three, so the oldest messages were deleted before they were read and the group restarted from the earliest available offset. The team raised retention to seven days for billing topics and added an alert on lag older than one hour. Common mistakes acks=1 for important data min.insync.replicas of 1 Too few partitions to scale consumers No alert on consumer lag Reassigning partitions without a throttle"},"data-platform/batch-orchestration-with-airflow":{"html":"\u003cp>Data jobs depend on each other and on time. An orchestrator records those dependencies, runs the jobs in order and lets you retry or rerun a day without guessing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>DAGs, tasks and schedules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Idempotent tasks and safe reruns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retries, timeouts and sensors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backfills and catchup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Operating the scheduler and workers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dags\">DAGs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A DAG is a graph of tasks with no cycles. Each run has a logical date, which is the data interval it processes, not the moment it started. Keep DAG files light, because the scheduler parses them often, and do the real work inside tasks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotent-tasks\">Idempotent tasks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A task is idempotent when running it twice for the same interval gives the same result. Write to a partition for the interval and overwrite it, or use merge, instead of appending blindly. Then a retry or a backfill never duplicates rows.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retries-and-sensors\">Retries and sensors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set retries with a delay and an execution timeout so a stuck task does not hold a slot forever. A sensor waits for a file or a table to arrive. Use deferrable sensors or reschedule mode so waiting does not occupy a worker.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"backfills\">Backfills\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A backfill reruns a range of past intervals after a fix or a new DAG. Limit concurrency with \u003ccode>max_active_runs\u003c/code> and pools so a backfill does not overload the source database. Because tasks are idempotent, rerunning old days is safe.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations\">Operations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The scheduler, the metadata database, the executor and the workers all need monitoring. Watch scheduler heartbeat, queued tasks, task duration and the age of the oldest running task. Run the metadata database with backups, because it holds run history.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"dag&gt;sch;sch&gt;wrk;wrk&gt;meta\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Batch orchestration with Airflow\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dag\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">DAG definition\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sch\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Scheduler\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wrk\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Workers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"meta\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Metadata database\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, DAG definition to Scheduler; 2, Scheduler to Workers; 3, Workers to Metadata database.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dag\">\u003cdt>DAG definition \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tasks and schedule.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Heavy parsing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sch\">\u003cdt>Scheduler \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Creates runs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Heartbeat lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wrk\">\u003cdt>Workers \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Slots full.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"meta\">\u003cdt>Metadata database \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run history.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not backed up.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-retry-that-doubled-yesterday-revenue\">Incident: A retry that doubled yesterday revenue\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A failed load task retried and appended rows a second time.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"psql -h warehouse -c &quot;select dt, count(*) from fact_sales where dt = '2026-11-20' group by dt&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>psql -h warehouse -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;select dt, count(*) from fact_sales where dt = &#x27;2026-11-20&#x27; group by dt&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> dt         | count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 2026-11-20 | 2408812\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The task appended rows each time it ran, so a retry after a timeout loaded the same day twice and the report showed double revenue. The team changed the task to overwrite the date partition, added a row count check against the source and marked the load task as idempotent in the DAG documentation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Append only tasks that cannot be retried\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Heavy imports at the top of DAG files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeouts on tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backfills with no concurrency limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No backup of the metadata database\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"dags","text":"DAGs"},{"id":"idempotent-tasks","text":"Idempotent tasks"},{"id":"retries-and-sensors","text":"Retries and sensors"},{"id":"backfills","text":"Backfills"},{"id":"operations","text":"Operations"},{"id":"incident-a-retry-that-doubled-yesterday-revenue","text":"Incident: A retry that doubled yesterday revenue"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Apache Airflow documentation","u":"https://airflow.apache.org/docs/"},{"t":"Airflow best practices","u":"https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/best-practices.html"},{"t":"dbt core","u":"https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt-core"}],"text":"Data jobs depend on each other and on time. An orchestrator records those dependencies, runs the jobs in order and lets you retry or rerun a day without guessing. What you will learn DAGs, tasks and schedules Idempotent tasks and safe reruns Retries, timeouts and sensors Backfills and catchup Operating the scheduler and workers DAGs A DAG is a graph of tasks with no cycles. Each run has a logical date, which is the data interval it processes, not the moment it started. Keep DAG files light, because the scheduler parses them often, and do the real work inside tasks. Idempotent tasks A task is idempotent when running it twice for the same interval gives the same result. Write to a partition for the interval and overwrite it, or use merge, instead of appending blindly. Then a retry or a backfill never duplicates rows. Retries and sensors Set retries with a delay and an execution timeout so a stuck task does not hold a slot forever. A sensor waits for a file or a table to arrive. Use deferrable sensors or reschedule mode so waiting does not occupy a worker. Backfills A backfill reruns a range of past intervals after a fix or a new DAG. Limit concurrency with max_active_runs and pools so a backfill does not overload the source database. Because tasks are idempotent, rerunning old days is safe. Operations The scheduler, the metadata database, the executor and the workers all need monitoring. Watch scheduler heartbeat, queued tasks, task duration and the age of the oldest running task. Run the metadata database with backups, because it holds run history. Batch orchestration with Airflow Flow in order: 1, DAG definition to Scheduler; 2, Scheduler to Workers; 3, Workers to Metadata database. Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. DAG definition Step What it does: Tasks and schedule. If it fails or is weak: Heavy parsing. Scheduler Service What it does: Creates runs. If it fails or is weak: Heartbeat lost. Workers Step What it does: Run tasks. If it fails or is weak: Slots full. Metadata database Data store What it does: Run history. If it fails or is weak: Not backed up. Incident: A retry that doubled yesterday revenue Incident A failed load task retried and appended rows a second time. $ psql -h warehouse -c \"select dt, count(*) from fact_sales where dt = &#x27;2026-11-20&#x27; group by dt\" dt | count 2026-11-20 | 2408812 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The task appended rows each time it ran, so a retry after a timeout loaded the same day twice and the report showed double revenue. The team changed the task to overwrite the date partition, added a row count check against the source and marked the load task as idempotent in the DAG documentation. Common mistakes Append only tasks that cannot be retried Heavy imports at the top of DAG files No timeouts on tasks Backfills with no concurrency limit No backup of the metadata database"},"data-platform/spark-and-distributed-processing-for-operators":{"html":"\u003cp>You do not need to write every Spark job to operate the platform that runs them. You need to know where time and memory go, and how to tell a bad query from a bad cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Driver, executors, stages and tasks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shuffles and why they are expensive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skew and small files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory, executors and dynamic allocation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading the Spark UI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-a-job-runs\">How a job runs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The driver turns code into a plan, splits it into stages and sends tasks to executors. A stage ends where data must be moved between machines. Narrow steps such as filters run in place, while wide steps such as joins and group by need a shuffle.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shuffles\">Shuffles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A shuffle writes data to disk, sends it over the network and reads it back. It is usually the slowest part of a job. Reduce it by filtering early, using broadcast joins for small tables and choosing the right number of shuffle partitions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"skew-and-small-files\">Skew and small files\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Skew means a few keys hold most of the data, so one task runs for an hour while the others finish in seconds. Salting keys or adaptive query execution can spread the load. Thousands of tiny files make planning and reading slow, so compact them into files of a few hundred megabytes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory-and-executors\">Memory and executors\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An executor out of memory is often caused by a skewed partition, a large collect to the driver or too many cores sharing one heap. Right size cores and memory per executor, use dynamic allocation to release idle executors and set spill and overhead memory with care.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-spark-ui\">The Spark UI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The stages tab shows task duration spread, shuffle read and write and spilled data. If the slowest task is far above the median, suspect skew. If many tasks spill to disk, memory is too small. If tasks wait for executors, the cluster is the limit, not the code.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"drv&gt;exe;exe&gt;shf;shf&gt;ui\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Spark and distributed processing for operators\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"drv\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Driver\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"exe\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Executors\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"shf\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shuffle\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ui\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Spark UI and metrics\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Driver to Executors; 2, Executors to Shuffle; 3, Shuffle to Spark UI and metrics.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"drv\">\u003cdt>Driver \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Plans the job.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Out of memory.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"exe\">\u003cdt>Executors \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run tasks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Lost executors.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"shf\">\u003cdt>Shuffle \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Moves data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skew.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ui\">\u003cdt>Spark UI and metrics \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Shows bottlenecks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-task-ran-for-three-hours\">Incident: One task ran for three hours\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly join was stuck on a single task while the rest of the cluster sat idle.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -s http://spark-history:18080/api/v1/applications/app-2026/stages/7/taskSummary | head -c 300\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -s http://spark-history:18080/api/v1/applications/app-2026/stages/7/taskSummary | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -c 300\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">{&quot;quantiles&quot;:[0.05,0.25,0.5,0.75,0.95],&quot;duration&quot;:[1200,1400,1900,2600,10800000]}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The median task took two seconds and the slowest took three hours because one customer id held most of the rows. Enabling adaptive skew handling and salting the key for that join spread the work and brought the stage down to a few minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Adding executors to fix a skewed join\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Collecting large data to the driver\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Thousands of small files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No history server or metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting shuffle partitions once for all jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"how-a-job-runs","text":"How a job runs"},{"id":"shuffles","text":"Shuffles"},{"id":"skew-and-small-files","text":"Skew and small files"},{"id":"memory-and-executors","text":"Memory and executors"},{"id":"the-spark-ui","text":"The Spark UI"},{"id":"incident-one-task-ran-for-three-hours","text":"Incident: One task ran for three hours"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Apache Spark documentation","u":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/"},{"t":"Spark tuning guide","u":"https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/tuning.html"},{"t":"Apache Iceberg documentation","u":"https://iceberg.apache.org/docs/latest/"}],"text":"You do not need to write every Spark job to operate the platform that runs them. You need to know where time and memory go, and how to tell a bad query from a bad cluster. What you will learn Driver, executors, stages and tasks Shuffles and why they are expensive Skew and small files Memory, executors and dynamic allocation Reading the Spark UI How a job runs The driver turns code into a plan, splits it into stages and sends tasks to executors. A stage ends where data must be moved between machines. Narrow steps such as filters run in place, while wide steps such as joins and group by need a shuffle. Shuffles A shuffle writes data to disk, sends it over the network and reads it back. It is usually the slowest part of a job. Reduce it by filtering early, using broadcast joins for small tables and choosing the right number of shuffle partitions. Skew and small files Skew means a few keys hold most of the data, so one task runs for an hour while the others finish in seconds. Salting keys or adaptive query execution can spread the load. Thousands of tiny files make planning and reading slow, so compact them into files of a few hundred megabytes. Memory and executors An executor out of memory is often caused by a skewed partition, a large collect to the driver or too many cores sharing one heap. Right size cores and memory per executor, use dynamic allocation to release idle executors and set spill and overhead memory with care. The Spark UI The stages tab shows task duration spread, shuffle read and write and spilled data. If the slowest task is far above the median, suspect skew. If many tasks spill to disk, memory is too small. If tasks wait for executors, the cluster is the limit, not the code. Spark and distributed processing for operators Flow in order: 1, Driver to Executors; 2, Executors to Shuffle; 3, Shuffle to Spark UI and metrics. Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Driver Step What it does: Plans the job. If it fails or is weak: Out of memory. Executors Step What it does: Run tasks. If it fails or is weak: Lost executors. Shuffle Data store What it does: Moves data. If it fails or is weak: Skew. Spark UI and metrics Observability What it does: Shows bottlenecks. If it fails or is weak: Ignored. Incident: One task ran for three hours Incident A nightly join was stuck on a single task while the rest of the cluster sat idle. $ curl -s http://spark-history:18080/api/v1/applications/app-2026/stages/7/taskSummary | head -c 300 {\"quantiles\":[0.05,0.25,0.5,0.75,0.95],\"duration\":[1200,1400,1900,2600,10800000]} (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The median task took two seconds and the slowest took three hours because one customer id held most of the rows. Enabling adaptive skew handling and salting the key for that join spread the work and brought the stage down to a few minutes. Common mistakes Adding executors to fix a skewed join Collecting large data to the driver Thousands of small files No history server or metrics Setting shuffle partitions once for all jobs"},"data-platform/data-quality-contracts-and-lineage":{"html":"\u003cp>Pipelines that run are not the same as data that is right. Quality checks, contracts and lineage make bad data visible before an executive reads it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What to test: freshness, volume, schema and values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Data contracts between producers and consumers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Lineage and the blast radius of a change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where to run checks and what to do on failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ownership and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-test\">What to test\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Freshness asks whether the table was updated on time. Volume asks whether the row count is within the normal range. Schema checks catch new, removed or retyped columns. Value checks cover nulls, duplicates, ranges and referential integrity. Start with a few high value checks on critical tables.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"contracts\">Contracts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A data contract is an agreement between the team that produces a dataset and the teams that use it. It states the schema, meaning of fields, freshness promise and how breaking changes are announced. Checking the contract in the producer pipeline stops a renamed column from reaching consumers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"lineage\">Lineage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Lineage records which datasets and jobs feed which others, for example with OpenLineage. When a source breaks, lineage tells you which dashboards and models are affected. Before changing a column it tells you who to warn.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-checks-run\">Where checks run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run checks at ingestion to reject or quarantine bad input, and after transformations to protect curated tables. On failure a pipeline can stop, quarantine the bad rows or publish with a warning, depending on how critical the table is. Decide this in advance for each table.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ownership-and-alerts\">Ownership and alerts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every important dataset has an owner and a channel. An alert that says only that a test failed is not enough: include the table, the failing check, recent values and a link to the runbook. Track the time to detect and the time to fix bad data.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"prod&gt;chk;chk&gt;lin;lin&gt;own\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Data quality, contracts and lineage\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Producer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chk\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Quality checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lin\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Lineage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"own\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Owner alert\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Producer to Quality checks; 2, Quality checks to Lineage; 3, Lineage to Owner alert.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Producer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owns the contract.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silent change.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chk\">\u003cdt>Quality checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Freshness, volume, schema.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too few checks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lin\">\u003cdt>Lineage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Finds consumers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"own\">\u003cdt>Owner alert \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Right person.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Nobody.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-renamed-column-that-broke-the-revenue-report\">Incident: A renamed column that broke the revenue report\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A producer renamed a column and the finance dashboard showed zeros.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -c &quot;print('null share of revenue_usd: 100 percent')&quot;\necho &quot;owner of finance.daily_revenue: unknown&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;print(&#x27;null share of revenue_usd: 100 percent&#x27;)&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">null share of revenue_usd: 100 percent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner of finance.daily_revenue: unknown&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A source team renamed amount to revenue_usd, the transformation selected the old name and filled it with nulls, and no check noticed. After the incident the team added a schema contract check in the producer pipeline, a null share test on the finance table and lineage so the producer could see which reports depended on the column.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Testing nothing until a user complains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Silent schema changes by producers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No owner for important tables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Alerts without context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating every check as a hard stop\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-to-test","text":"What to test"},{"id":"contracts","text":"Contracts"},{"id":"lineage","text":"Lineage"},{"id":"where-checks-run","text":"Where checks run"},{"id":"ownership-and-alerts","text":"Ownership and alerts"},{"id":"incident-a-renamed-column-that-broke-the-revenue-report","text":"Incident: A renamed column that broke the revenue report"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Great Expectations","u":"https://github.com/great-expectations/great_expectations"},{"t":"OpenLineage","u":"https://github.com/OpenLineage/OpenLineage"},{"t":"dbt core","u":"https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt-core"}],"text":"Pipelines that run are not the same as data that is right. Quality checks, contracts and lineage make bad data visible before an executive reads it. What you will learn What to test: freshness, volume, schema and values Data contracts between producers and consumers Lineage and the blast radius of a change Where to run checks and what to do on failure Ownership and alerts What to test Freshness asks whether the table was updated on time. Volume asks whether the row count is within the normal range. Schema checks catch new, removed or retyped columns. Value checks cover nulls, duplicates, ranges and referential integrity. Start with a few high value checks on critical tables. Contracts A data contract is an agreement between the team that produces a dataset and the teams that use it. It states the schema, meaning of fields, freshness promise and how breaking changes are announced. Checking the contract in the producer pipeline stops a renamed column from reaching consumers. Lineage Lineage records which datasets and jobs feed which others, for example with OpenLineage. When a source breaks, lineage tells you which dashboards and models are affected. Before changing a column it tells you who to warn. Where checks run Run checks at ingestion to reject or quarantine bad input, and after transformations to protect curated tables. On failure a pipeline can stop, quarantine the bad rows or publish with a warning, depending on how critical the table is. Decide this in advance for each table. Ownership and alerts Every important dataset has an owner and a channel. An alert that says only that a test failed is not enough: include the table, the failing check, recent values and a link to the runbook. Track the time to detect and the time to fix bad data. Data quality, contracts and lineage Flow in order: 1, Producer to Quality checks; 2, Quality checks to Lineage; 3, Lineage to Owner alert. Outside party Observability Data store Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Producer Outside party What it does: Owns the contract. If it fails or is weak: Silent change. Quality checks Observability What it does: Freshness, volume, schema. If it fails or is weak: Too few checks. Lineage Data store What it does: Finds consumers. If it fails or is weak: Missing. Owner alert Service What it does: Right person. If it fails or is weak: Nobody. Incident: A renamed column that broke the revenue report Incident A producer renamed a column and the finance dashboard showed zeros. $ python -c \"print(&#x27;null share of revenue_usd: 100 percent&#x27;)\" null share of revenue_usd: 100 percent $ echo \"owner of finance.daily_revenue: unknown\" (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) A source team renamed amount to revenue_usd, the transformation selected the old name and filled it with nulls, and no check noticed. After the incident the team added a schema contract check in the producer pipeline, a null share test on the finance table and lineage so the producer could see which reports depended on the column. Common mistakes Testing nothing until a user complains Silent schema changes by producers No owner for important tables Alerts without context Treating every check as a hard stop"},"data-platform/governance-cost-and-reliability-of-data-platforms":{"html":"\u003cp>A data platform stores the most sensitive and the most expensive data in the company. Governance, cost and reliability decide whether people can trust and afford it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Access control and sensitive data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Retention, deletion and privacy requests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cost control for storage and compute\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reliability targets for data products\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Platform operations and on call\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"access-and-sensitive-data\">Access and sensitive data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Classify data as public, internal or sensitive and apply rules to each class. Use roles and groups instead of per person grants, column level and row level controls for sensitive fields, and masking or tokenisation for identifiers. Log who reads sensitive tables and review the logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retention-and-deletion\">Retention and deletion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep data only as long as there is a reason to. Set retention per dataset, and make deletion work through every copy: raw files, derived tables, backups and caches. A privacy deletion request must be answerable with a list of where the person data lives, which lineage helps to build.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost\">Cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Storage cost grows quietly: duplicate copies, old partitions and tiny files. Compute cost grows with unbounded queries and always on clusters. Use lifecycle rules to move cold data to cheaper storage, auto stop idle clusters, set query limits and show each team its own bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reliability\">Reliability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Treat important datasets as products with service levels: freshness within a time, completeness above a threshold and a stated recovery time. Measure them, report on them and spend an error budget the same way you would for a service. Keep pipelines idempotent so recovery is a rerun.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operations\">Operations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run an on call rotation for the platform with runbooks for stuck pipelines, lagging streams and failed loads. Review incidents without blame, track repeat causes and invest in the ones that cost the most analyst time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"gov&gt;cost;cost&gt;slo;slo&gt;ops\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Governance, cost and reliability of data platforms\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gov\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Governance rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cost\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cost controls\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slo\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Data SLOs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ops\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform on call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Governance rules to Cost controls; 2, Cost controls to Data SLOs; 3, Data SLOs to Platform on call.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gov\">\u003cdt>Governance rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Access and retention.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Over broad access.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cost\">\u003cdt>Cost controls \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Lifecycle and limits.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Runaway queries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slo\">\u003cdt>Data SLOs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Freshness and recovery.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unmeasured.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ops\">\u003cdt>Platform on call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runbooks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-query-that-cost-more-than-the-team-monthly-budget\">Incident: A query that cost more than the team monthly budget\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>One analyst unbounded query scanned a petabyte of raw logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bq query --dry_run --use_legacy_sql=false 'select count(*) from raw.events'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bq query --dry_run --use_legacy_sql=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;select count(*) from raw.events&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Query successfully validated. This query will process 1048576000000000 bytes.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>There was no limit on bytes scanned and the raw table was not partitioned, so a full scan ran against the entire history. The platform team added a per user scan limit, partitioned the table by date, required a partition filter and showed dry run estimates in the query tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Granting access per person instead of by role\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deleting data from only one copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No lifecycle rules on object storage\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No per query cost limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No stated reliability target for key tables\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"access-and-sensitive-data","text":"Access and sensitive data"},{"id":"retention-and-deletion","text":"Retention and deletion"},{"id":"cost","text":"Cost"},{"id":"reliability","text":"Reliability"},{"id":"operations","text":"Operations"},{"id":"incident-a-query-that-cost-more-than-the-team-monthly-budget","text":"Incident: A query that cost more than the team monthly budget"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Data mesh principles","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/data-mesh-principles.html"},{"t":"OpenLineage","u":"https://github.com/OpenLineage/OpenLineage"},{"t":"Apache Iceberg documentation","u":"https://iceberg.apache.org/docs/latest/"}],"text":"A data platform stores the most sensitive and the most expensive data in the company. Governance, cost and reliability decide whether people can trust and afford it. What you will learn Access control and sensitive data Retention, deletion and privacy requests Cost control for storage and compute Reliability targets for data products Platform operations and on call Access and sensitive data Classify data as public, internal or sensitive and apply rules to each class. Use roles and groups instead of per person grants, column level and row level controls for sensitive fields, and masking or tokenisation for identifiers. Log who reads sensitive tables and review the logs. Retention and deletion Keep data only as long as there is a reason to. Set retention per dataset, and make deletion work through every copy: raw files, derived tables, backups and caches. A privacy deletion request must be answerable with a list of where the person data lives, which lineage helps to build. Cost Storage cost grows quietly: duplicate copies, old partitions and tiny files. Compute cost grows with unbounded queries and always on clusters. Use lifecycle rules to move cold data to cheaper storage, auto stop idle clusters, set query limits and show each team its own bill. Reliability Treat important datasets as products with service levels: freshness within a time, completeness above a threshold and a stated recovery time. Measure them, report on them and spend an error budget the same way you would for a service. Keep pipelines idempotent so recovery is a rerun. Operations Run an on call rotation for the platform with runbooks for stuck pipelines, lagging streams and failed loads. Review incidents without blame, track repeat causes and invest in the ones that cost the most analyst time. Governance, cost and reliability of data platforms Flow in order: 1, Governance rules to Cost controls; 2, Cost controls to Data SLOs; 3, Data SLOs to Platform on call. Security Observability Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Governance rules Security What it does: Access and retention. If it fails or is weak: Over broad access. Cost controls Observability What it does: Lifecycle and limits. If it fails or is weak: Runaway queries. Data SLOs Observability What it does: Freshness and recovery. If it fails or is weak: Unmeasured. Platform on call Service What it does: Runbooks. If it fails or is weak: No owner. Incident: A query that cost more than the team monthly budget Incident One analyst unbounded query scanned a petabyte of raw logs. $ bq query --dry_run --use_legacy_sql= false &#x27;select count(*) from raw.events&#x27; Query successfully validated. This query will process 1048576000000000 bytes. (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) There was no limit on bytes scanned and the raw table was not partitioned, so a full scan ran against the entire history. The platform team added a per user scan limit, partitioned the table by date, required a partition filter and showed dry run estimates in the query tool. Common mistakes Granting access per person instead of by role Deleting data from only one copy No lifecycle rules on object storage No per query cost limit No stated reliability target for key tables"}});